From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 11:14:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: g16: Optionally ring compute doorbells
 once per group

Every compute record rings the compute channel within its own publish
pass: with g16_compute_onebell 2 the first record of a pass rings at
once and the pass end rings for the rest. Each doorbell starts a
firmware scheduler call. At depth 8 on 1.1089, with g16_submit_defer 2
and g16_submit_kick 16, that was 1.15 compute records per doorbell. On
1.1093, ringing render doorbells once per group of 4 renders (0.26 rings
per render) took light drawing at depth 8 down by 3.2 us per render and
at depth 64 by 2.1 us.

Add g16_compute_bell_group (runtime 0644, default 0; 2 to 16). With k,
a compute publication made while at least k compute commands are in
flight posts its record without a doorbell, and the k-th such
publication rings for all of them. A publication with fewer than k in
flight rings as before, and any compute ring covers the owed records.
At the end of every publish pass, owed records are rung once fewer
than k compute commands are in flight, so a record is never left
unrung: at most k - 1 are owed at any time. Both firmware compute
queues share the one compute channel, so there is one group for the
engine. Records that register a queue always ring. After a fail-stop
nothing is rung. A stats line counts rings, deferred records, group
payments and end-of-pass payments. Nothing changes while the word is 0.

The rule is in a core-only module with host tests.

Assisted-by: LLM
